From 4dee219a9f4c1c8d8e48f273aeade9f779581260 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuubOS <nuubos@localhost>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 15:54:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Run the AICA on a thread of its own (nuubOS)

The sound chip took a quarter to a third of the emulation thread in heavy
games (Shenmue II on the RG CubeXX: mixing 13 %, DSP 9 %, stream and
envelope steps, the ARM7). On the console the ARM7 and the SH4 run side
by side and talk through sound RAM and the AICA registers, so with
threaded rendering the AICA now runs on its own thread, a tick of 32
samples at a time and never ahead of the SH4: each AICA tick of SH4 time
asks for one more, and at vblank the emulation thread waits until all
asked for are made (libAICA_Sync), so between frames (save states,
reset, the frame's audio) it is as before.

The SH4 now sees the AICA up to a tick (0.7 ms) late. The registers are
shared under aica_lock (the SH4 takes it for each access, the AICA thread
for each sample); interrupts the AICA raises for the SH4 are passed on by
the emulation thread at the next tick; CD audio sectors are read ahead
by the emulation thread (cdda_prefetch), reading the disc being the
GD-ROM's business.

Shenmue II, Hong Kong street, 640x480: 36 -> 55-60 fps.

Upstream: not submitted (nuubOS H700 tuning).
